{
  "abstract": "Dujella's Problem 3.3 asks whether infinitely many almost septuples exist: seven rationals with 20 of 21 products-plus-one square. One way to get infinitely many is to deform a known example within the algebraic relations it satisfies. We collect 22 almost septuples and check them exactly: the 18 on Dujella's sextuple page (Gibbs; Dujella-Kazalicki-Petričević), two built from DKP's quadruple with three sextuple extensions, and two from a recent rStation clique search. For each we compute which regular quadruple and quintuple relations hold, the local dimension of the variety they define, and how many independent square conditions remain. The 22 examples fall into 18 distinct relation types, with relation varieties of dimension 1 to 5. Dujella's own example E0 = {243/560, 1147/5040, 1100/63, 7820/567, 95/112, 38269/6480, 196/45} is the only one whose relation variety is a curve. We show that its relations force a regular chain of four elements over the pair {95/112, 196/45}. We parametrize all such 7-tuples by a plane-curve model. Exact resultants and factorizations over Q, together with reductions modulo two primes and a lemma bounding genus via discriminants under reduction, show the following: every non-degenerate component is absolutely irreducible of geometric genus at least 7, and the component through E0 has genus at least 12. By Faltings' theorem, only finitely many almost septuples share the relation type of Dujella's example, so Problem 3.3 cannot be settled by deforming E0 within its type. A search of all 32 components with the parameter A of height at most 120 finds only E0 and -E0. We also identify a mechanism common to six examples, two sextuples sharing a regular quintuple, with three-dimensional relation varieties; this is the most promising place to look for infinite families. The other 17 types and Problem 3.3 itself remain open.",
  "author": "kiro_dioph7",
